schools across the u.s are struggling to  stay open while most are offering  in-person instruction more than 4 400  k-12 schools shifted to remote learning  or closed for at least one day last week  mainly due to covid19 related staffing  issues what happens then is the school  says okay well let's let's find a  substitute for the teacher who isn't  there but the problem is that that has  also created a shortage among  substitutes to help with the school  staffing crunch some states are stepping  in the greatest need right now today is  substitute teachers dr kurt steinhaus is  among the new mexico state officials who  spearheaded the supporting teachers and  families initiative which asks state  workers and national guard members to  volunteer to become licensed as  substitute teachers these national guard  members are community members their  coaches their parents they know  their communities  since winter break around 60 school  districts and charter schools in new  mexico have moved into remote learning  dr steinhaus said the state is short  more than a thousand substitutes less  than a week after the program was  announced 46 national guard members were  already deployed to schools these  national guard members who are  substituting they're not just in there  just randomly doing something they're  following the classroom teachers lesson  plan other states have taken similar  steps  oklahoma governor kevin stitt signed an  executive order last tuesday allowing  state workers to retain their regular  jobs while working in the classroom  since then more than 200 state employees  have signed up to become substitute  teachers they'll be able to step in with  assistance from the schools and be able  to continue that learning process in  california governor gavin newsom signed  an order aimed at reducing the barriers  for districts to hire short-term  substitute teachers we just did an  executive order this week to allow  flexibility with substitutes and  continue to allow retirees to come back  to help us with staffing shortages in  the immediate to get through the next  few weeks because we must keep our kids  in person safely in school connecticut  has also cleared a path for retired  teachers to fill in by allowing them to  work without losing retirement benefits  the kansas state board of education  temporarily eased requirements for  getting an emergency substitute license  and in late december michigan governor  gretchen whitmer signed a bill to allow  school secretaries and other staff to  work as substitutes while it's great to  find a substitute for a teacher who's  not there the ideal scenario is for the  teacher him or herself to be there as  elected officials and school  administrators push to continue  in-person learning many acknowledge that  relying on substitute teachers is not  the perfect solution substitute teachers  perform a valuable role but the fact of  the matter is they are not the classroom  teacher who knows the kids and knows  where they are with the material you  should automatically know that that  equals  five the staff shortages at both the  teacher and the sub level i think the  combination is  is raising some fresh concerns about  kids maybe falling behind in their  education at a time when there is  already widespread agreement that remote  learning was bad for a lot of kids when  it comes to the academics while the  statewide top-down approaches are  intended to address the immediate need  for teachers it will take time before  schools get back to pre-pandemic  staffing levels even as covid19 cases  decline in some parts of the country  there's no sense of mission accomplished  where we're in the clear but there is  this sense that we can get back to  something that  more resembles normal which they want  for their kids who have had anything but  that for  almost two years now
 

